1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,020
Say you ask an AI agent to swap 500 USDC.

2
00:00:04,060 --> 00:00:05,320
That request is an intent.

3
00:00:05,360 --> 00:00:07,340
Before it can safely settle onchain,

4
00:00:07,380 --> 00:00:10,301
it has to pass three security gates in our system.

5
00:00:10,341 --> 00:00:11,863
Let's walk through them.

6
00:00:12,913 --> 00:00:15,938
First, the model doesn't touch the chain directly.

7
00:00:15,978 --> 00:00:18,620
It works inside an execution harness.

8
00:00:18,660 --> 00:00:20,628
The model makes one typed call,

9
00:00:20,668 --> 00:00:22,149
and the harness does the rest:

10
00:00:22,189 --> 00:00:25,041
it reads chain state, stages the transaction,

11
00:00:25,081 --> 00:00:27,381
simulates it, and commits it.

12
00:00:28,281 --> 00:00:30,006
Why do we need gates at all?

13
00:00:30,046 --> 00:00:33,570
A financial agent works a lot like a coding agent:

14
00:00:33,610 --> 00:00:36,658
it takes an intent, builds something, and ships it.

15
00:00:36,698 --> 00:00:39,584
But code is reviewed and tested before it deploys,

16
00:00:39,624 --> 00:00:42,196
while a transaction is only signed.

17
00:00:42,236 --> 00:00:45,830
Along the way it can be attacked, or simply built wrong.

18
00:00:45,870 --> 00:00:48,071
And once it settles, it's final,

19
00:00:48,111 --> 00:00:52,037
even when it's valid onchain but wrong for the user.

20
00:00:56,087 --> 00:00:58,624
At the build step, we simulate the transaction

21
00:00:58,664 --> 00:01:01,794
against a fork of the live chain, again and again.

22
00:01:01,834 --> 00:01:04,987
When a simulation fails, the error goes back to the model,

23
00:01:05,027 --> 00:01:07,935
which fixes the transaction and tries again.

24
00:01:07,975 --> 00:01:10,315
Only a transaction that passes is committed.

25
00:01:10,355 --> 00:01:13,880
We call this gate 0.5, because it runs inside the harness,

26
00:01:13,920 --> 00:01:16,313
while the agent is still building.

27
00:01:19,564 --> 00:01:21,567
Once the agent has built the transaction,

28
00:01:21,607 --> 00:01:25,248
it still can't sign it, because it never holds the key.

29
00:01:25,288 --> 00:01:27,872
Instead, it sends a signing request to a separate

30
00:01:27,912 --> 00:01:30,798
wallet module running in a secure enclave.

31
00:01:30,838 --> 00:01:34,048
That module checks the request against the user's policy,

32
00:01:34,088 --> 00:01:35,163
such as spending limits,

33
00:01:35,203 --> 00:01:38,716
allowed contracts and recipients, and only then signs.

34
00:01:38,756 --> 00:01:40,255
This is gate one.

35
00:01:42,605 --> 00:01:44,643
Gate two runs onchain.

36
00:01:44,683 --> 00:01:47,244
A guard contract on the user's account checks

37
00:01:47,284 --> 00:01:50,646
every transaction against rules the user has set,

38
00:01:50,686 --> 00:01:53,943
like a sanctions blocklist or a spending cap.

39
00:01:53,983 --> 00:01:57,460
If a transaction breaks a rule, the contract reverts it,

40
00:01:57,500 --> 00:01:59,627
even if the signer approved it.

41
00:02:00,477 --> 00:02:03,131
Finally, the signed transaction waits in the

42
00:02:03,171 --> 00:02:06,110
mempool until a block builder picks it up.

43
00:02:06,363 --> 00:02:08,169
As the builder packs the block,

44
00:02:08,209 --> 00:02:10,085
it checks each transaction against

45
00:02:10,125 --> 00:02:11,768
the protocol's assertions.

46
00:02:11,808 --> 00:02:12,709
If one fails,

47
00:02:12,749 --> 00:02:15,426
the builder removes it before the block is sealed.

48
00:02:15,466 --> 00:02:17,406
This is the last gate.

49
00:02:18,056 --> 00:02:19,966
Only then is the block added to the chain,

50
00:02:20,006 --> 00:02:22,168
and the transaction is settled.

51
00:02:23,618 --> 00:02:25,157
Each gate sees something different,

52
00:02:25,197 --> 00:02:27,776
so no single gate can catch everything.

53
00:02:28,626 --> 00:02:30,629
Put together, every risk is stopped

54
00:02:30,669 --> 00:02:32,441
at the checkpoint that can see it.

55
00:02:32,481 --> 00:02:36,016
Simulation catches wrong decimals and stale routes.

56
00:02:36,056 --> 00:02:38,733
The signing policy catches a swapped payload,

57
00:02:38,773 --> 00:02:40,916
and partly a forged instruction.

58
00:02:40,956 --> 00:02:43,505
The onchain guard blocks a policy breach,

59
00:02:43,545 --> 00:02:45,224
like a sanctioned recipient.

60
00:02:45,264 --> 00:02:47,685
And the builder drops a sandwich attack.

61
00:02:47,725 --> 00:02:51,221
Only a transaction that passes every gate settles.

62
00:02:52,471 --> 00:02:55,241
This idea goes beyond our own implementation.

63
00:02:55,281 --> 00:02:57,837
It applies to any financial workflow.

64
00:02:58,337 --> 00:03:01,478
The human's identity is canonical and persistent;

65
00:03:01,518 --> 00:03:03,765
it's the account that holds the real money.

66
00:03:03,805 --> 00:03:06,029
From it, the user authorizes sessions,

67
00:03:06,069 --> 00:03:09,593
which let agents act only on approved resources.

68
00:03:09,633 --> 00:03:13,100
Each session's authority is scoped, capped, and expires.

69
00:03:13,140 --> 00:03:14,876
The same checks work onchain,

70
00:03:14,916 --> 00:03:17,512
at a bank, or on an exchange.

71
00:03:17,552 --> 00:03:20,421
You can think of it as OAuth for money.

72
00:03:23,371 --> 00:03:25,142
That's how Aomi takes an agent's

73
00:03:25,182 --> 00:03:27,244
transaction from intent to settlement.

74
00:03:27,284 --> 00:03:30,641
You can read the full research at aomi.dev.
